ENTRIES.append(dict(
title="6. Does “Be Ye Angry, and Sin Not” Mean Anger Is Allowed? Psalm 4:4 and Ephesians 4:26",
answer="Paul does not license anger. “Be ye angry, and sin not” takes the line of Psalm 4:4 and sets a limit on it: do not sin, and do not let the sun go down on your wrath. The KJV renders the psalm “Stand in awe, and sin not,” and Paul’s wording matches the older Greek translation of the psalm. What he commands the Body is to put away bitterness, wrath and anger (4:31) and to give the devil no place (4:27).",
stand=[
"Psalm 4 is David’s evening prayer. He is pressed by men who turn his glory into shame, and he tells them to stop and think: “commune with your own heart upon your bed, and be still.” Ephesians 4:17–32 is Paul’s instruction to the Body on the new man: put off the old man, speak truth, and let no corrupt word come out of the mouth.",
"The verse stands between “speak every man truth with his neighbour” (4:25) and “Neither give place to the devil” (4:27). The audience is the saints at Ephesus.",
],
rows=[
("Ps 4:4","Ps 4:4","“Stand in awe, and sin not: commune with your own heart upon your bed, and be still.”"),
("Eph 4:25–27","Eph 4:25–27","Truth, then “Be ye angry, and sin not,” then “Neither give place to the devil.”"),
("Eph 4:31","Eph 4:31","“Let all bitterness, and wrath, and anger, and clamour … be put away from you.”"),
],
carry=[
("Stated","Paul gives two limits in one sentence: “and sin not,” and “let not the sun go down upon your wrath” (Ephesians 4:26)."),
("Stated","five verses later he tells the same readers to put away “bitterness, and wrath, and anger” (4:31). The command in 4:26 and the command in 4:31 stand in one passage."),
("Shown by the context","the psalm line has the same first limit: “sin not.” Psalm 4:4 in the KJV reads “Stand in awe,” and the psalm goes on to “be still.” The line is a restraint in both places."),
("Inferred","that Paul takes the line as the Greek translation of the psalm reads it. That translation reads the verse as <i>be angry, and sin not</i>, and Paul’s wording is the same. The letter does not say that he is quoting."),
("Inferred","that the anger Paul allows is the passing flash of an offence, with no room left to settle. “Give place to the devil” in the next verse names what happens when it is kept."),
],
other=[
"<b>Anger is allowed.</b> Many popular readings take the verse as permission: some anger is righteous and the believer may keep it, provided he does not sin. This reading must explain 4:31, and the limit that the sun may not go down on it.",
"<b>The psalm is a command to tremble.</b> Some read the Hebrew of Psalm 4:4 as <i>tremble</i> or <i>be agitated</i> and Paul as using the psalm loosely. This reading must explain why Paul’s sentence is in the form of the Greek translation.",
"<b>A limited concession with a short fuse.</b> The reading taken here: Paul grants that a man may be moved to anger and forbids sin in it and delay in letting it go.",
],
rtbl=[
("Anger is allowed","The believer may stay angry.","Ephesians 4:31; the sunset limit."),
("The Hebrew sense, “tremble”","Paul is loose with the psalm.","His wording matches the Greek translation."),
("A limited concession","Anger may rise; it may not sin or stay.","None; it joins 4:26 and 4:31."),
],
means=[
"The reader may be moved to anger and is not told that the feeling is itself sin. He is told that anger carried past the day gives place to the devil, and that the Body’s answer to wrong is to put anger away and forgive as God in Christ forgave him (4:32).",
],
related="<b>Companions:</b> Ephesians; Psalms.",
))

