ENTRIES.append(dict(
title="7. Why Does Paul Say Christ “Gave Gifts” When Psalm 68:18 Says “Received”? Ephesians 4:8",
answer="Paul reads the line of a victor who comes up the hill with captives and then distributes what he took. The psalm says “thou hast received gifts for men” and Ephesians says “gave gifts unto men.” Both describe the same victory from different ends: the king takes tribute and the king gives it out. Paul applies the line to the ascended Christ, who gives apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers to the Body (4:11).",
stand=[
"Psalm 68 is a processional psalm. God arises and his enemies are scattered, he goes forth before his people, and the hill of God is the hill of Bashan; “the chariots of God are twenty thousand” and he ascends “on high” (68:15–18). It is Israel’s psalm of God coming to dwell at Zion, and it closes with kings bringing presents (68:29).",
"Ephesians 4:7–13 stands inside Paul’s teaching on the unity of the Body: “unto every one of us is given grace according to the measure of the gift of Christ.” The quotation is introduced by “Wherefore he saith.”",
],
rows=[
("Ps 68:17–18","Ps 68:17–18","God ascends “on high … led captivity captive … received gifts for men; yea, for the rebellious also.”"),
("Eph 4:7–10","Eph 4:7–10","“Gave gifts unto men”; the ascent and the descent “into the lower parts of the earth.”"),
("Eph 4:11–13","Eph 4:11–13","What he gave: apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers."),
("Judg 5:12","Judg 5:12","“Lead thy captivity captive,” in Deborah’s victory song."),
("Col 2:15","Col 2:15","“Having spoiled principalities and powers … triumphing over them in it.”"),
],
carry=[
("Stated","the verb differs: “received” in the psalm and “gave” in Ephesians. Paul does not remark on the difference."),
("Shown by the context","the psalm line has a victor who takes captives and gifts; “for men” can mean from men and to be given on to them. A king who receives tribute commonly gives it out, and Ephesians 4 goes straight to what he gave."),
("Shown by the context","“led captivity captive” is also used in Deborah’s song of victory over Israel’s enemy (Judges 5:12), so it names a victor’s train of prisoners."),
("Stated","Paul says the one who ascended is the one who “descended first into the lower parts of the earth” and “ascended up far above all heavens” (4:9–10). The letter does not say what “the lower parts of the earth” is, and does not say who the captives were."),
("Inferred","that Paul applies the victor’s line to Christ’s ascension and the giving of the gifts of 4:11. He does not say the psalm was written of Christ alone; its own subject is God ascending to Zion."),
("Inferred","that the Body’s gifts are Paul’s teaching on the Body and are not Israel’s psalm fulfilled. A shared line is not a shared audience, and the letter’s gifts are given to build up the Body (4:12)."),
],
other=[
"<b>Paul misquotes the psalm.</b> Some say Paul altered the text. He does not claim to quote the Hebrew; the sense of the line holds in both forms.",
"<b>Paul follows a different text.</b> Some commentaries say Paul used a Jewish paraphrase that read “gave.” This reading must show that such a text existed, and the letter does not mention it.",
"<b>The victor receives and gives.</b> The reading taken here: both verbs describe one triumph, and Paul stresses the second because the Body’s gifts come from it.",
],
rtbl=[
("Paul misquotes","He changed “received” to “gave.”","He draws no attention to the change."),
("A different text","Paul used a paraphrase.","The letter names no source."),
("Receive and give","One triumph, two ends.","None; it joins both verbs."),
],
means=[
"The reader is told where the Body’s gifts come from: the ascended Christ, who gives them for the work of the ministry and the building of the Body. The verse does not explain the “lower parts of the earth,” and the reader is not asked to build a doctrine on it.",
],
related="<b>Commentary Series:</b> Prophecy &amp; Mystery Commentary, Volume 2 (The Mystery Program); Pauline Doctrine Series, Volume 4 (The Assembly in Order). <b>Companions:</b> Ephesians; Psalms.",
))

