ENTRIES.append(dict(
title="8. Does “A Thousand Years Is as One Day” Explain the Delay? Psalm 90:4 and 2 Peter 3:8",
answer="Peter does not give a formula for counting time. He answers scoffers who say “Where is the promise of his coming?” by saying that God does not reckon as men do, and that the delay is longsuffering, “not willing that any should perish.” Psalm 90 says the same of God’s eternity against man’s brief life. Neither passage says that a day in prophecy is a thousand years or that the thousand years of Revelation 20 are a day.",
stand=[
"2 Peter 3 opens with “This second epistle, beloved, I now write unto you” and says the purpose is to stir up their minds to remember what the prophets and the apostles said (3:1–2). Scoffers will come “in the last days” and ask where the promise is. Peter answers from creation and the flood (3:5–7), then gives the verse.",
"Psalm 90 is <i>A Prayer of Moses</i>. It begins with God as “our dwelling place in all generations,” sets his everlasting against man’s turning to destruction, and then asks God to teach us to number our days (90:12).",
],
rows=[
("Ps 90:1–4","Ps 90:1–4","“From everlasting to everlasting”; “a thousand years in thy sight are but as yesterday.”"),
("Ps 90:12","Ps 90:12","“So teach us to number our days.”"),
("2Pet 3:3–4","2Pet 3:3–4","Scoffers: “Where is the promise of his coming?”"),
("2Pet 3:8–9","2Pet 3:8–9","“One day is with the Lord as a thousand years”; “not slack” and “longsuffering.”"),
("Rev 20:6","Rev 20:6","“Reign with him a thousand years.”"),
],
carry=[
("Stated","Peter puts the saying in two directions: a day is as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day (3:8). The line is a statement about the Lord’s relation to time."),
("Stated","the next verse gives the point: “The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness” (3:9)."),
("Shown by the context","Psalm 90 uses the thousand years to say that God is not bound by time; the verse says that a thousand years “when it is past” is “as yesterday.” It does not give a ratio for a prophecy."),
("Shown by the context","Peter’s letter is written to Israel’s believers scattered abroad (1 Peter 1:1) and its readers are told to remember what the holy prophets said; the argument of chapter 3 is that the promise stands."),
("Inferred","that the verse is no basis for turning a day in prophecy into a thousand years, or a thousand years into a day. Peter applies it to the delay and states the reason for the delay in the next sentence."),
("Inferred","that it does not decide the length of the thousand years in Revelation 20:6. Revelation names a thousand years as a stated period; 2 Peter 3:8 does not mention it."),
],
other=[
"<b>A day-age formula.</b> Some use the verse to say that each day of creation, or each prophetic day, equals a thousand years. This reading must explain why Peter states the saying in both directions and ties it to the delay.",
"<b>The thousand years in Revelation 20 are symbolic.</b> Some use 3:8 to read Revelation’s thousand years as not literal. This reading must explain why Peter says nothing of Revelation.",
"<b>God is not bound by time.</b> The reading taken here: the verse answers the scoffers and ends in longsuffering, as Psalm 90 ends in a prayer for wisdom.",
],
rtbl=[
("A day-age formula","One day equals a thousand years.","Peter’s saying runs both ways."),
("Revelation’s thousand years are symbolic","3:8 shows the number is not literal.","Peter does not mention Revelation 20."),
("God is not bound by time","The verse answers delay with longsuffering.","None; it follows 3:9."),
],
means=[
"The reader is told that the delay of the promised coming is not neglect, and that God’s patience has a stated purpose in repentance. The verse is not a calendar and does not date anything.",
],
related="<b>Digital Studies:</b> Study 87, The Last Days, reports 2 Peter 3:3 as written; this entry argues a position on one verse. <b>Commentary Series:</b> Prophecy &amp; Mystery Commentary, Volume 3 (The Kingdom Program). <b>Companions:</b> 2 Peter; Psalms.",
))

