ENTRIES.append(dict(
title="9. What Is the Rest in Psalm 95 and Hebrews 3–4?",
answer="The rest is the rest God sware Israel’s fathers would not enter because of unbelief, and Hebrews says a rest still “remaineth … to the people of God.” The warning in Hebrews is written to Hebrew believers, set in the story of Israel in the wilderness, and it is not a warning to the Body. Psalm 95:7–11 is the text; Hebrews uses its “To day” to say that the offer is open while it is called today.",
stand=[
"Psalm 95 begins with a call to worship and ends with a warning: “To day if ye will hear his voice, Harden not your heart.” Hebrews 3 quotes it in full and builds three chapters on it (3:7–4:11). The readers are those who confess “the Apostle and High Priest of our profession” (3:1) and are called “holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly calling.”",
"Numbers 14 is the event behind the psalm: the spies, the people’s refusal, and God’s oath that those who provoked him would not see the land. Hebrews 3:16–19 recalls the same event.",
],
rows=[
("Num 14:21–23","Num 14:21–23","“They shall not see the land which I sware unto their fathers.”"),
("Ps 95:7–11","Ps 95:7–11","“To day if ye will hear his voice, Harden not your heart … they should not enter into my rest.”"),
("Heb 3:7–11","Heb 3:7–11","The psalm quoted, introduced by “the Holy Ghost saith.”"),
("Heb 3:18–19","Heb 3:18–19","“They could not enter in because of unbelief.”"),
("Heb 4:1–3","Heb 4:1–3","“A promise being left us of entering into his rest.”"),
("Heb 4:8–9","Heb 4:8–9","“If Jesus had given them rest … There remaineth therefore a rest.”"),
],
carry=[
("Stated","the oath was “They shall not enter into my rest,” sworn of those who tempted him in the wilderness (Psalm 95:9–11; Hebrews 3:9–11)."),
("Stated","Hebrews gives the reason they did not enter: “unbelief” (3:19), and the word they heard “did not profit them, not being mixed with faith” (4:2)."),
("Stated","“If Jesus had given them rest” (4:8) names Joshua, in the Greek form of the name. The rest he gave was not the final one, for God “would … afterward have spoken of another day.”"),
("Shown by the context","the readers are Hebrews who share the “heavenly calling” and are tempted to draw back (3:1, 12). The wilderness generation is the warning, and the audience is the people to whom Moses spoke."),
("Inferred","that the rest is the rest promised in the land, and the “another day” is the fuller rest of the age to come. The letter says “a rest” (4:9), a word it does not use elsewhere, and does not say what the rest is made of."),
("Inferred","that the warning does not pass to the Body. The Body’s hope is stated in other letters, and nothing in Hebrews addresses it. The letter's covenant language is Israel’s."),
],
other=[
"<b>Salvation rest for every believer.</b> Many read the rest as the believer’s present rest in Christ and the warning as against losing it. This reading must explain why the letter looks back to the land and forward to “another day.”",
"<b>Heaven.</b> Some read the rest as heaven. This reading must explain why Hebrews roots it in the land and Joshua.",
"<b>A rest for Israel, still ahead.</b> The reading taken here: the rest is the rest God promised his people, not yet entered, and the warning is given to Hebrew believers on the edge of it.",
],
rtbl=[
("Salvation rest now","Any believer rests in Christ.","The land; “another day.”"),
("Heaven","The rest is the believer’s home.","Joshua and the wilderness."),
("A rest for Israel, still ahead","The rest is the promised rest.","None; it follows the letter’s audience."),
],
means=[
"The reader hears a warning against hardening the heart, in a letter that is addressed to Hebrews. The reader keeps the Body’s assurance where Paul states it and does not turn Hebrews 3–4 into a warning to the Body.",
],
related="<b>Digital Studies:</b> Study 82, The Kinsman-Redeemer, reports Hebrews as written. <b>Commentary Series:</b> Covenantal Commentary, Volume 4 (The Eternal Purpose of God). <b>Companions:</b> Hebrews; Numbers; Psalms.",
))

