ENTRIES.append(dict(
title="10. What Does “This Day Have I Begotten Thee” Mean in Psalm 2:7, Acts 13:33 and Hebrews 1:5?",
answer="The line is God’s declaration to the anointed king, and the New Testament sets it at the point where the Son is shown to be the Son: the resurrection and the enthronement. Paul quotes it at Antioch in Pisidia as the second psalm’s promise, Hebrews puts it above the angels, and Romans 1:4 says he was “declared to be the Son of God with power … by the resurrection from the dead.” The text does not say he became Son on that day.",
stand=[
"Psalm 2 is a royal psalm. The kings of the earth set themselves against “his anointed,” God sets his king on Zion, and the king is told to ask for the nations. Acts 4:25–26 quotes the opening verses. Acts 13:14–41 gives Paul’s message in the synagogue at Antioch, quoting Psalm 2:7 as the promise to the fathers that God has fulfilled “in that he hath raised up Jesus again.”",
"Hebrews 1:5 asks to which of the angels God said “Thou art my Son,” and Hebrews 5:5 uses the same line of the priesthood. Romans 1:3–4 joins the Son’s descent from David and the declaration by resurrection.",
],
rows=[
("Ps 2:1–2","Ps 2:1–2","The nations rage “against the LORD, and against his anointed.”"),
("Ps 2:7–9","Ps 2:7–9","“Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee … the heathen for thine inheritance.”"),
("Ac 13:32–34","Acts 13:32–34","“Raised up Jesus again; as it is also written in the second psalm.”"),
("Heb 1:5","Heb 1:5","“For unto which of the angels said he at any time”"),
("Heb 5:5","Heb 5:5","“Christ glorified not himself to be made an high priest.”"),
("Rom 1:3–4","Rom 1:3–4","“Declared to be the Son of God with power … by the resurrection.”"),
],
carry=[
("Stated","Paul says God fulfilled the promise made to the fathers by raising up Jesus, “as it is also written in the second psalm” (Acts 13:32–33)."),
("Stated","Paul separates two sayings in the same sermon: the second psalm line, and “as concerning that he raised him up from the dead, now no more to return to corruption” (13:34), which is a further word about the same resurrection."),
("Stated","Romans 1:4 says the Son was “declared” to be the Son of God by the resurrection, not made so by it."),
("Shown by the context","Psalm 2 is about a king set on Zion with the nations as his inheritance, so the line is a statement of the king’s status. The New Testament quotes it three times of Christ and in each case ties it to his exaltation."),
("Inferred","that “this day” in the psalm is the day of the king’s installation and, in Acts 13, the day of the resurrection. The text names no hour and does not say the Son began on that day."),
("Inferred","that Paul’s use at Antioch is audience-specific: <i>Paul’s synagogue preaching was audience-specific, not commission-specific.</i> Paul’s commission never changes: he is the apostle of the Gentiles, and his own gospel is the gospel of the grace of God. When he reasons in the synagogues from Israel’s Scriptures, he proclaims Israel’s Messiah and kingdom hope to Israel, whose prophetic program remained operative until its national suspension at Acts 28. That is audience-specific proclamation, not a second gospel and not a change of commission; it does not make Paul a Prophecy apostle, and it does not make his hearers members of the Body by that message."),
],
other=[
"<b>The Son became Son at the resurrection.</b> Some read “this day” as the day he began to be Son. This reading must explain Hebrews 1, which sets the Son above the angels before any day, and Romans 1:4, which says “declared.”",
"<b>An eternal begetting.</b> Many creeds read the line of the eternal relation of the Father and the Son. This reading must explain why Acts 13 and Romans 1 tie it to the resurrection.",
"<b>A declaration of the Son at his exaltation.</b> The reading taken here: the line declares who he is at the point of his resurrection and enthronement.",
],
rtbl=[
("He became Son at the resurrection","“This day” is his beginning.","Hebrews 1:5; “declared.”"),
("An eternal begetting","The line speaks of eternity.","Acts 13:33; Romans 1:4."),
("A declaration at exaltation","“This day” is when he was shown to be Son.","None; it fits all three texts."),
],
means=[
"The reader may hold both: the Son is the Son, and the resurrection is where God declared it with power. The line is the Father’s word to the king set on Zion, and the New Testament shows its fulfilment in the one raised from the dead.",
],
related="<b>Commentary Series:</b> Acts Commentary, Volume 3 (Paul Called and Sent); Gospel Commentary, Volume 2 (The Lord’s Ministry to Israel). <b>Companions:</b> Acts; Psalms; Hebrews; Romans.",
))
